  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe current optimization method relies on local search from multiple starting points, which may get stuck in local minima. We address this by using a multi-resolution approach combined with a hybrid optimization strategy that leverages both global and local search. The core idea is to start with a coarse grid using a population-based optimizer (Differential Evolution) for global exploration, then refine the solution on a finer grid using a gradient-based local optimizer. This approach balances exploration and exploitation, leveraging the fact that solutions on coarser grids can serve as good initial points for finer grids while ensuring we don't miss the global minimum. The key bottleneck addressed is the limited exploration of the solution space by local methods alone.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe minimize the overlap integral C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx over h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with \u222b h(x) dx = 1. Discretization uses n_points samples over [0,2] with dx=2.0/n_points. The objective is computed as (max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')))*dx. The solution vector h must satisfy 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2. The optimization uses:\n- Global phase: Differential Evolution with penalty for constraint violation (penalty coefficient 1000)\n- Local phase: SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and equality constraint \u2211h = n_points/2\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent solution available, use it as one initial point; otherwise generate random points\n   - For n=188 (coarse grid): 1000 evaluations with Differential Evolution\n   - For n=376 (fine grid): Interpolate best coarse solution, adjust integral, then 10000 evaluations with SLSQP\n\n2. Objective Function:\n   - For each candidate h, compute correlation = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n   - C_5 = (max(correlation)) * (2.0/n_points)\n   - Penalize constraint violation: total_penalty = 1000 * |\u2211h - n_points/2|\n\n3. Global Optimization (Differential Evolution):\n   - Population size: 100\n   - Bounds: [(0,1)]*n_points\n   - Mutation factor: 0.8, crossover probability: 0.7\n   - Track best solution (lowest C_5 + total_penalty)\n\n4. Fine Grid Refinement (SLSQP):\n   - Interpolate coarse solution to 376 points using linear interpolation\n   - Scale to satisfy \u2211h = 376/2\n   - Optimize with SLSQP bounds [0,1] and constraint \u2211h = 376/2\n\n5. Comparison:\n   - Compare best coarse and fine solutions, return the one with lowest C_5\n\nSearch Choices and Useful Variations\n- Coarse grid: 1000 evaluations, population size 100\n- Fine grid: 10000 evaluations, local refinement\n- Initial population: Mix parent solution (if available) with random points\n- Global optimizer: Differential Evolution with penalty\n- Local optimizer: SLSQP with bounds and equality constraint\n- Time budget: 500 seconds for coarse grid, 500 seconds for fine grid\n\nComputebudget and Robustness\n- Coarse grid: ~500 seconds (1000 DE evaluations)\n- Fine grid: ~500 seconds (10000 SLSQP evaluations)\n- Dominant cost: Correlation computations (O(n_points^2))\n- Fallback: Return best solution found if time expires\n- Constraint handling: Penalize constraint violations heavily\n- Objective consistency: Re-evaluate final candidate with actual metric\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n- Feasibility: Check 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and |\u2211h - n_points/2| < 1e-5\n- Objective: Recompute C_5 using np.correlate with mode='full'\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Integral adjustment: Linear interpolation followed by scaling\n- Objective computation: (max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (2.0/n_points))",
